# Level 3 — Complex (30 prompts)

### C01 — Three-finger gripper, pivot-driven
Design a three-finger mechanical gripper assembly with a Ø90 mm circular mounting flange, 8 mm flange thickness, and four Ø8 mm bolt holes on an Ø70 mm pitch circle. Add three identical fingers arranged 120° apart around the central axis; each finger is 75 mm from pivot centre to tip, 12 mm wide, and 8 mm thick, with a Ø8 mm pivot hole. Include three support ears, three connecting links, and a central actuator slider with a Ø16 mm guide. Keep each part separate, define consistent pivot axes and clearances, and state any assumed linkage geometry. Do not claim synchronized motion unless the actual joints and motion have been validated.

### C02 — Two-stage parallel-axis gearbox
Create a gearbox assembly with a 180 × 130 × 12 mm base plate, two parallel shafts separated by 90 mm, and a separate two-piece housing. Stage one uses a 20-tooth module-2 spur gear driving a 40-tooth gear; stage two uses a 16-tooth module-2 gear driving a 32-tooth gear on the output shaft. Use a consistent 20° pressure angle and 12 mm face width. Add four bearing seats sized for nominal Ø20 mm and Ø25 mm bearings, shaft shoulders, keys, and a bolted cover. Preserve separate gears, shafts, bearings as simplified solids, housing and fasteners; verify shaft spacing against gear centre distances.

### C03 — Lead-screw linear actuator
Create an actuator with a 250 × 70 × 12 mm base, two parallel guide rods Ø12 mm spaced 45 mm apart, a central nominal Ø16 mm lead screw, and a travelling carriage 70 × 60 × 25 mm. Place the carriage on two guide bushings and include a nut block with a nominal 16 mm screw interface. Add end supports with coaxial screw-bearing bores and mounting holes. Model the screw thread as a simplified helix or a documented cylindrical representation if true threads are unsupported. Keep parts separate, align axes, and provide a 120 mm nominal carriage travel envelope.

### C04 — Belt-driven pulley transmission
Create an assembly with a motor shaft Ø12 mm, a 60 mm pitch-diameter driving pulley, a 120 mm pitch-diameter driven pulley, and a 400 mm centre distance. Use a nominal 10 mm-wide belt path and show the belt as a separate closed loop or a clearly labelled simplified representation. Include two shaft supports on a common base plate and keyways in both pulleys. Keep shafts parallel, pulleys coplanar, and the belt path clear of the shafts and supports. State that the belt geometry is an envelope unless a manufacturable tooth profile is implemented.

### C05 — Universal joint assembly
Create a cross-type universal joint connecting two Ø20 mm shafts at a 30° angle. Include two yokes, a central cross/spider with four Ø8 mm trunnions, and four bearing-cap cylinders. Each yoke has two ears 8 mm thick with aligned Ø8 mm bearing holes and a 20 mm clear gap. Include shaft clamps or keyed hubs. Keep the yoke axes intersecting at the spider centre, trunnions coaxial with their bearing holes, and all parts separately identifiable.

### C06 — Bench vise assembly
Design a bench vise with a 180 × 100 × 20 mm base, fixed jaw 100 mm wide and 45 mm tall, moving jaw of matching width and height, and a nominal 20 mm lead screw providing 100 mm travel. Add two guide bars Ø16 mm spaced 60 mm apart, a separate screw nut block, a thrust collar and a 120 mm-long handle with end knobs. Provide mounting slots in the base and replaceable jaw plates with two mounting screws each. Show a fully open and a closed configuration or one clear assembly state with an indicated travel envelope.

### C07 — Four-bar linkage mechanism
Create a planar four-bar linkage mounted to a 140 × 50 × 8 mm base. Fixed pivot centres are 80 mm apart. Use a 35 mm input crank, a 100 mm coupler and a 70 mm output rocker, each 8 mm thick with Ø8 mm end holes. Use Ø8 mm shoulder pins and 2 mm axial spacers. Arrange a valid, non-colliding initial pose in the plane and include 1 mm side clearance between moving links and supports. Preserve components and explicitly define the pivot centres; if no kinematics engine exists, validate only the initial geometry and linkage closure.

### C08 — Rack-and-pinion linear actuator
Build a rack-and-pinion assembly with a module-2, 20-tooth pinion, 12 mm face width, and Ø12 mm shaft bore. Create a 180 mm-long straight rack with matching module and nominal 12 mm face width, plus a guide housing and two mounting ears. Set the pinion axis perpendicular to rack travel and locate the centre distance according to the selected gear geometry. Add a shaft support with Ø12 mm bore and a keyway in the pinion hub. Keep teeth meshed without visibly penetrating each other; state whether teeth are true involutes or approximate geometry.

### C09 — Bearing-supported roller conveyor module
Design a short conveyor roller module 500 mm long overall with four rollers Ø50 mm × 350 mm, spaced 100 mm apart. Each roller has a 3 mm shell wall, end hubs, and Ø12 mm axle. Create two side rails 500 × 50 × 8 mm, with aligned axle mounting holes; add cross-members between the rails and foot mounting plates. Keep rollers parallel and equidistant, axles coaxial with their rail holes, and rotating envelopes free of rail interference. Keep the rollers, axles, rails and frame separate.

### C10 — Pneumatically actuated two-jaw gripper
Create a two-jaw parallel gripper with a 100 × 70 × 12 mm base, a compact cylinder with Ø25 mm bore and 40 mm stroke, and two jaw sliders guided symmetrically around the centreline. Each jaw has a 60 × 20 × 12 mm body and replaceable 4 mm-thick gripping pad. Use mirrored slotted links to convert piston motion into opposing jaw translation; specify pin diameters and pivot locations. Model a valid closed and open position or one state plus the travel envelope, and keep jaws, links, pins, cylinder and base as separate components.

### C11 — Multi-piece pump housing with impeller
Create a centrifugal pump assembly with a two-piece casing, Ø100 mm impeller envelope, Ø25 mm shaft, two bearing supports, and inlet/outlet pipe interfaces. The casing has a volute-style chamber surrounding the impeller, a Ø40 mm inlet eye and a tangential Ø32 mm outlet passage. Include a rear cover, shaft seal region and bolt holes around the casing perimeter. Keep casing halves, impeller, shaft and covers separate. Validate solid geometry and passage continuity where supported; do not claim calculated flow performance without CFD or validated hydraulic analysis.

### C12 — Small planetary gearbox
Create a planetary gear assembly with a sun gear of 20 teeth, three planet gears of 15 teeth each, and an internal ring gear of 50 teeth; use module 1.5 mm, 20° pressure angle, and 10 mm face width. Confirm tooth-count and centre-distance compatibility before generating geometry; if the selected values are inconsistent for the intended arrangement, report the issue and propose a corrected set instead of forcing an invalid mesh. Include a carrier plate, three planet pins, input shaft, ring housing and output interface. Preserve each gear as a separate component and define coaxial axes and equal planet spacing.

### C13 — Gear-driven scissor lift
Create a tabletop scissor-lift assembly with a 200 × 150 mm base and a matching top platform. Use two crossed link pairs, each link 160 mm long, 20 mm wide and 8 mm thick, with Ø8 mm pivot holes. Add sliding end pins in 10 mm-wide slots, a central lead-screw actuator, screw nut block and four guide features. Provide a low-height state with no link self-intersection and clearly defined pivots and sliders. A complete motion study is only required if the software includes kinematics; otherwise verify the selected pose and travel clearances.

### C14 — Four-station rotary indexing table
Create a rotary indexing table with a Ø250 mm top plate and four workholding stations spaced at 90°. Each station has a 60 × 50 mm mounting grid with four Ø8 mm holes. Support the table on a Ø100 mm central bearing, a 20 mm vertical shaft and a base housing 180 × 180 × 40 mm. Add an indexing ring with four radial detents, a separate locking pin, and a simplified drive crank/link. Define the four station coordinates, avoid interference with the housing, and preserve table, shaft, base, detent ring and lock components.

### C15 — Shaft-mounted disc brake assembly
Design a disc brake assembly around a Ø20 mm shaft. Create a brake rotor Ø160 mm × 8 mm with a Ø20 mm hub bore and four Ø8 mm mounting holes on a Ø50 mm pitch circle. Add a caliper body, two opposing friction pads, two guide pins and a mounting bracket attached to a 100 × 60 × 10 mm base. Centre the pads on the rotor friction annulus and keep appropriate assembly gaps without intersecting the rotor. Include a caliper opening that permits rotor installation; do not calculate braking performance unless supported by an explicit analysis.

### C16 — Chain-and-sprocket transmission
Create two parallel-shaft sprockets connected by a simplified roller chain. Use 12-tooth and 24-tooth sprockets with a shared nominal chain pitch of 12.7 mm and a shaft centre distance of 250 mm. Provide 12 mm-wide tooth regions, Ø12 mm and Ø20 mm shaft bores respectively, plus keyways. Represent the chain as linked rollers and plates or a simplified loop with clearly documented approximation. Ensure both sprockets share a plane and the chain path does not intersect the shafts or surrounding frame.

### C17 — Ball-screw supported CNC axis
Design a compact CNC linear axis with a 400 × 100 × 12 mm base, two Ø16 mm linear guide rails spaced 70 mm apart, four bearing blocks, and a central ball-screw envelope Ø16 mm. Add a 100 × 80 × 20 mm moving carriage, end support blocks with coaxial screw seats, and a nut housing attached to the carriage. Provide 250 mm nominal travel and end-stop positions. Keep moving carriage and blocks aligned to the rails, represent the screw thread realistically if supported, and validate collision-free motion envelopes or clearly identify motion as unverified.

### C18 — Adjustable camera/inspection gimbal
Create a two-axis gimbal supporting a 100 × 80 × 60 mm instrument housing. Include a base plate 140 × 120 × 8 mm, a yaw ring around a vertical Ø12 mm pivot, and a pitch yoke with two Ø10 mm coaxial pivots. Add clamp knobs, bearing sleeves and hard stops to limit yaw to ±90° and pitch to ±45°. Keep axes orthogonal and intersecting at the intended rotation centre; preserve the housing, yoke, ring, pivots and base as separate components.

### C19 — Screw-driven pipe clamp
Create a pipe clamp for a nominal Ø60 mm tube. Use a 180 mm-long base, two uprights, a fixed semicircular saddle radius 32 mm, and a moving pressure shoe with a matching concave contact face. Include a vertical Tr16-style screw represented by a nominal shaft, a rotating handle and a swivel pad; set a maximum clamp opening of 40 mm. Add four base mounting holes and rounded edges at the contact shoe. Keep screw and shoe coaxial, the clamping travel unobstructed, and the pipe contact region free of unintended interference.

### C20 — Robotic wrist flange with cable routing
Design a three-part robotic wrist module with a Ø100 mm robot-side flange, a central hollow shaft Ø35 mm OD/Ø25 mm ID, and a tool-side flange Ø80 mm. Use six Ø8 mm mounting holes on an Ø80 mm pitch circle on the robot flange and four Ø6.5 mm holes on an Ø60 mm pitch circle on the tool flange. Add a bearing housing, retaining shoulder, and an internal cable passage through the rotating axis. Maintain concentricity across the shaft and flanges and keep the cable channel continuous.

### C21 — Modular fixture plate and locating system
Create a 300 × 200 × 20 mm fixture plate with a 25 mm grid of Ø10 mm reamed holes, omitting holes that fall within 15 mm of the plate perimeter. Add four separate locating pins Ø10 mm × 20 mm high, two adjustable supports with M12 nominal threaded stems, and two clamp arms with pivot pins. Include a workpiece block 120 × 80 × 25 mm located against two datum faces. Ensure pins engage matching plate holes, clamps reach the workpiece without collisions, and each fixture component remains separate.

### C22 — Compact hydraulic power unit layout
Create a simplified hydraulic power unit assembly with a 250 × 180 × 10 mm base, a motor cylindrical envelope Ø100 mm × 180 mm, a pump housing, a 1.5 L rectangular reservoir 180 × 100 × 90 mm, two pipe ports and an independent filter canister. Add four motor mounting slots and an aligned motor-pump coupling guard. Arrange the components with service clearances and accessible fasteners; use separate solids for functional components. Treat the reservoir as a closed-wall volume, and label all plumbing as layout geometry rather than pressure-rated design.

### C23 — Small compressor piston-crank assembly
Create a simplified single-cylinder compressor mechanism with a cylinder bore Ø40 mm, stroke 35 mm and connecting rod centre distance 75 mm. Include a crankshaft with crank throw 17.5 mm, piston Ø39.5 mm, piston pin Ø8 mm, connecting rod with Ø8 mm eye holes, and a cylinder block with mounting feet. Add a head plate with inlet and outlet port openings. Place the crank, rod and piston in a geometrically consistent mid-stroke configuration, preserve moving parts separately, and verify the crank throw agrees with half the stroke.

### C24 — Camshaft with three followers
Create a 220 mm-long camshaft with three cam lobes spaced along its axis and angularly phased at 0°, 120° and 240°. Each lobe has a 24 mm base-circle diameter and 5 mm nominal lift; use a smooth sinusoidal lift profile if the CAD system supports profile-based cam geometry, otherwise clearly label an approximate lobe profile. Add two Ø20 mm bearing journals, a Ø12 mm drive end and three separate roller followers Ø16 mm on Ø8 mm axles. Position each follower tangent to its lobe and keep follower axes parallel.

### C25 — Vertical-axis wind turbine rotor assembly
Create a simplified vertical-axis rotor with three curved blades spanning 400 mm in height and 180 mm maximum rotor diameter. Use a central shaft Ø20 mm, top and bottom end plates Ø160 mm × 8 mm, and three equally spaced blade supports at 120°. Each blade is 2 mm thick with a defined curved profile and end attachment tabs. Add two shaft bearings and a base frame. Keep all blades aligned, preserve separate components, and identify aerodynamic performance as unverified without analysis.

### C26 — Filter housing with replaceable cartridge
Create a filter housing with a cylindrical chamber Ø120 mm internal diameter and 180 mm internal height, a 10 mm-thick bolted lid and a bottom base. Add inlet and outlet ports Ø25 mm oriented tangentially at two different elevations, a removable filter cartridge Ø80 mm × 150 mm, and a central sealing stem Ø12 mm. Use eight lid fasteners on a Ø140 mm bolt circle and an annular gasket groove. Keep fluid volume connected around the cartridge as intended, with removable parts and no accidental blocked ports.

### C27 — Two-axis worm-driven positioning stage
Create an XY positioning stage with two stacked 180 × 160 × 12 mm plates, each driven by a separate screw-and-nut mechanism. The lower stage travels 80 mm along X and the upper stage travels 60 mm along Y. Add four guide rods Ø12 mm per stage and bearing blocks, with two orthogonal nominal Ø12 mm lead screws. Keep the upper stage carried by the lower moving stage, maintain guide parallelism and screw alignment, and provide distinct base, carriages, rods, screws, nuts and supports.

### C28 — Automotive suspension corner mock-up
Create a simplified suspension corner assembly for packaging evaluation: a hub carrier, hub with five-lug Ø12 mm bolt holes on a Ø114.3 mm pattern, a coil-over strut, lower control arm, upper link and steering tie rod. Use a Ø60 mm hub centre, 280 mm nominal vertical distance between upper/lower suspension hard points, and a 250 mm control-arm span. Define pivot axes and separate parts; represent wheel/brake envelopes as separate simplified solids. Ensure components do not overlap unrealistically in the static pose. Do not claim roadworthiness or validate suspension dynamics.

### C29 — Twin-cylinder reciprocating pump crank assembly
Design a two-cylinder pump mechanism with cylinders spaced 100 mm apart, each Ø32 mm bore and 30 mm stroke. Use a shared crankshaft with 15 mm crank throws phased 180° apart, two connecting rods of 65 mm centre distance, pistons Ø31.5 mm, and two cylinder blocks on a common 220 × 100 mm base. Add a front bearing support, rear bearing support, and separate head plates with two valve-port holes each. Place the pistons in consistent crank positions and verify each throw equals half the stroke.

### C30 — Modular robotic end-effector tool changer
Create a tool changer with a robot-side plate Ø100 mm × 12 mm, a tool-side plate Ø90 mm × 12 mm, three tapered locating pins, three matching receptacles, and a central locking sleeve Ø40 mm. Add four Ø8 mm mounting holes on the robot plate and six Ø6.5 mm holes on the tool plate. Include a simplified cam-lock ring and two air/electrical utility pass-throughs. Define an alignment scheme that prevents incorrect rotational assembly and preserve plates, locators, sleeve, locking ring and fasteners as separate components.

