# Level 4 — Highly Complex (20 prompts)

### H01 — Three-finger synchronized gripper with actuator
Design a complete three-finger gripper using a Ø100 mm mounting flange, four Ø8 mm robot-interface holes on a Ø80 mm pitch circle, and a central Ø25 mm actuator guide. Each of the three identical fingers has a 90 mm reach, replaceable 4 mm-thick pad, two-link pivoted motion, and a Ø8 mm finger pivot. A central translating actuator with 30 mm stroke drives a common synchronizing plate connected to all three fingers through equal-length links. Arrange fingers at 120° intervals. Provide an open and closed assembly configuration, consistent link lengths and axes, separate editable components, and collision/clearance checks. If the mechanism cannot be proven to synchronize, report it as a geometric concept rather than validated kinematics.

### H02 — Two-stage helical gearbox with housing and bearings
Design a two-stage helical gearbox with an input speed-reduction ratio target of approximately 6:1. Use stage ratios 2:1 and 3:1; choose compatible tooth counts, module, helix angle, centre distance, face width and shaft layout rather than forcing incompatible arbitrary values. Include input, intermediate and output shafts, six gears as applicable to the selected layout, keyed hubs, bearing pairs, spacers, seals, a split casing, bolted cover, drain plug and mounting feet. Make gear axes and helix directions consistent for the intended shaft arrangement. Provide a component tree, an exploded arrangement and a closed housing assembly. Label gear contact and transmission performance unverified unless calculated.

### H03 — Automated pick-and-place linkage head
Create a compact pick-and-place head with a base plate, two servo mounting envelopes, a parallelogram linkage, a vertical guided slide, and a two-jaw gripper. Specify a 160 mm horizontal reach and 80 mm vertical travel. Use 8 mm-thick links with reamed Ø8 mm pivot holes and shoulder pins; define all pivot centre distances so the parallelogram remains geometrically consistent. Add linear guide blocks, actuator brackets, cable-clearance envelopes and end stops. Preserve each component and produce a collision-free reference pose. If motion simulation is unavailable, identify the assembly as a kinematic layout, not a verified automated cycle.

### H04 — Compact CNC spindle cartridge
Design a spindle cartridge around an Ø20 mm tool interface and a 150 mm-long spindle shaft. Include two spaced angular-contact bearing envelope seats, a cylindrical cartridge housing, a drawbar passage Ø8 mm, a nose flange Ø90 mm, and a drive pulley or motor interface at the rear. Include a front labyrinth/seal region, bearing retainers, spacers and a removable nose cap. Define concentric axes and shoulder stacks; avoid interference between shaft, bearings and housing. Provide separate parts and a sectioned inspection configuration. Do not claim spindle-speed, thermal or bearing-life suitability without additional calculations.

### H05 — Four-axis robot arm mechanical mock-up
Create a four-axis articulated robot mock-up with a 200 × 200 × 20 mm base, a shoulder joint, elbow joint, wrist rotation and tool flange. Use nominal arm-link lengths of 250 mm and 220 mm; each link should be a hollow structural body with 4 mm walls. Include bearing housings, shaft interfaces, motor-envelope cylinders, cable-routing passages and hard stops. Define joint axes and a reachable reference pose with no self-collision. Keep links, joints, covers and tool flange as separate components. Treat reach and interference as geometric checks only unless actual kinematics is implemented.

### H06 — Hydraulic vise with synchronized moving jaws
Design a hydraulic vise assembly with a 250 × 120 × 25 mm base, two opposing jaws, a 160 mm maximum opening, and a pair of Ø25 mm hydraulic cylinder envelopes. Include two guide rails, four guide blocks, jaw plates with replaceable serrated inserts, a fluid manifold and hose-port geometry. Specify how both jaws are synchronized through a rack-and-pinion or mechanically linked mechanism; choose one approach and model the required parts. Preserve independent components and check the entire open and closed travel envelope. Do not claim clamping force without pressure and structural calculations.

### H07 — Differential gearbox assembly
Create an automotive-style bevel differential with a ring gear, pinion shaft, differential carrier, two side gears, two spider gears, cross pin, two output shafts, bearing envelopes and a split housing. Set a nominal final-drive ratio of 4:1 by selecting a compatible ring/pinion tooth-count pair. Define bevel gear axes, gear centre relationships and carrier clearances; model gear teeth realistically where supported or clearly mark approximate teeth. Keep gear and shaft components separate. Provide a sectioned view and check for basic geometric interferences. Do not claim accurate tooth contact or vehicle performance unless the selected gear geometry is fully validated.

### H08 — Cam-indexed assembly station
Design a mechanical indexing station with a four-position rotary table Ø240 mm, a cam-driven follower mechanism, a locking pin, a geared input shaft, two bearing supports and a rigid 300 × 300 mm base frame. Define four table stops 90° apart and include adjustable station fixtures with 50 × 40 mm mounting grids. Use separate rollers and follower axes, maintain a clear tool zone over each station, and add guards around the drive envelope. Model one valid index position and the critical interfaces. Describe the intended index sequence; mark timing and impact loads unverified unless simulation/calculation is performed.

### H09 — Screw compressor rotor pair and casing concept
Create a simplified twin-screw compressor geometry with two intermeshing helical rotor envelopes, parallel axes, 100 mm rotor length, and a surrounding split housing. Include inlet and outlet chambers, bearing seats at both ends, seal regions, drive-end shafts and bolted covers. Use a consistent rotor centre distance and ensure the two helical envelopes do not grossly interpenetrate. If a true conjugate screw profile is beyond the backend, generate a clearly labelled geometric concept and report the limitation rather than presenting it as manufacturable. Export the split housing and rotor assembly separately.

### H10 — Multi-cylinder cranktrain assembly
Design an inline four-cylinder cranktrain concept with four cylinder bores Ø80 mm spaced 95 mm centre-to-centre, 70 mm stroke, 140 mm connecting-rod centre distance, four pistons, four rods and a common crankshaft. Include a crankcase envelope, main-bearing saddles, main caps, front pulley and flywheel. Phase crank throws consistently for an inline-four configuration and place pistons at geometrically consistent positions. Preserve moving components and provide a sectioned inspection view. Check that rods, crank webs and cylinder envelopes avoid obvious interference; do not claim a complete engine or combustion simulation.

### H11 — Robotic gripper with interchangeable fingers and quick-change mount
Create a robot-mounted gripper with a circular interface Ø100 mm, a central actuator, a three-finger synchronized linkage and an interchangeable tool tip on each finger. Each tool tip must be removable using two M6 nominal fasteners and a locating shoulder. Add three finger-base carriers, pivot pins, connecting links, actuator slider, a synchronizing spider and a protective cover. Define a datum-based interface so replacement tips preserve their contact position. Include open/closed poses, component-level exports, collision checks and a clear assembly tree. Do not assume gripping-force capability without material and load calculations.

### H12 — Planetary gearbox with selectable carrier arrangement
Create a compact planetary transmission with a sun gear, three planet gears, an internal ring gear and a carrier. Target a 4:1 reduction in one configuration; determine a feasible tooth-count set and indicate which member is input, output and fixed. Include input/output shafts, a ring housing, carrier pins, bearings, spacers and a bolted casing. Verify tooth-count compatibility, equal planet spacing, coaxial axes and gear centre distances before generating teeth. Provide separate parts and a sectional assembly. If gear meshing is approximated, state that the ratio is kinematic intent rather than verified involute contact.

### H13 — Ball-screw XY gantry with Z head
Design a compact XYZ CNC gantry with a 450 × 350 mm base envelope, X travel 300 mm, Y travel 200 mm and Z travel 100 mm. Use paired guide rails on X and Y, a central ball-screw envelope per axis, bearing supports, nut mounts, a moving bridge, a Z carriage and a Ø65 mm spindle envelope. Choose rail spacings and mounting patterns that support the stated travel without rail-block run-off. Define nested moving coordinate frames so Y rides on X and Z rides on the bridge. Include end stops and motor-mount envelopes. Check travel limits and collision envelopes; stiffness and machining accuracy remain unverified without analysis.

### H14 — Automated material handling gripper with pneumatic circuit layout
Create an end-effector assembly with a three-finger or parallel-jaw grip module, a rotary actuator envelope, two pneumatic cylinders, hose routing and a robot mounting flange. Use a 120 mm mounting flange, Ø8 mm interface holes on an Ø90 mm pitch circle and clearances for two 8 mm-OD hoses. Model valves and fittings as simplified component envelopes, not internal functional valve mechanisms. Route hoses without passing through solid parts or bending to zero radius, include strain-relief brackets, and produce a component tree and assembly export. Explicitly distinguish CAD routing validation from pneumatic performance validation.

### H15 — Centrifugal pump serviceable assembly with seal stack
Design a serviceable centrifugal pump with a volute casing, removable front cover, Ø100 mm impeller, Ø25 mm shaft, motor coupling, two bearing supports and a mechanical-seal stack. Inlet bore is Ø40 mm and outlet bore is Ø32 mm; include a casing drain port Ø8 mm at the lowest point of the selected orientation. Add a bolted casing joint, gasket groove, shaft shoulders, seal seat and coupling keyway. Ensure the impeller has assembly clearance inside the casing and the flow passages connect. Generate an exploded service view and validate geometry, not hydraulic performance or seal life.

### H16 — Compact machine-tool turret indexing assembly
Create an eight-station tool turret with a Ø220 mm indexing face and eight tool-holder bores Ø20 mm arranged on a Ø160 mm pitch circle. Include a central spindle, bearing stack, index lock pin, detent ring, drive gear, base housing and bolted tool holders. Define one indexed position per 45° and a locking interface that aligns the station bore with a fixed tool axis. Ensure the turret can rotate within the housing envelope and does not interfere with the lock pin when unlocked. Keep the parts separately editable and provide a sectional configuration; index repeatability is unverified without tolerancing and motion analysis.

### H17 — Compact internal-combustion engine valvetrain mock-up
Create a single-cylinder four-valve cylinder-head valvetrain mock-up containing a cylinder head envelope, four valve stems, four valve springs, two camshafts, cam lobes, followers and timing gears. Set valve stem diameter to 6 mm, valve head diameter to 24 mm for intake and 20 mm for exhaust, and nominal lift to 8 mm. Arrange valves at practical non-intersecting angles around a central cylinder axis and locate the cam lobes over their followers. Add bearing journals and a timing cover. This is a packaging model; do not claim combustion, thermal, airflow or fatigue validation.

### H18 — Mechanical press linkage and guarded frame
Design a small mechanical press with a 350 × 250 mm base, a rigid portal frame with 250 mm clear width, a guided ram with 100 mm stroke, and a crank-slider or toggle drive. Include input shaft, bearings, connecting link, ram guides, tool-holder plates and a removable safety guard envelope. Select link lengths and pivots so the ram remains constrained to vertical motion through the specified stroke. Provide top/bottom positions and confirm no obvious geometric interference with the frame. Keep load capacity and safety compliance out of scope unless separate structural and risk analyses are carried out.

### H19 — Modular automated inspection fixture
Create a fixture for inspecting a prismatic component 150 × 90 × 40 mm. Use a 300 × 220 × 20 mm base plate, three datum supports, two side locators, two pneumatic clamps, a vertical probing-column envelope and four interchangeable sensor brackets. Add 25 mm-grid mounting holes, Ø8 mm locator pins, clamp pivot joints and adjustment slots. Define a repeatable 3-2-1 locating scheme without overconstraining the workpiece, preserve component-level editability, and route the sensor brackets so the probe reaches six specified measurement zones. Report reach/clearance checks separately from measurement uncertainty or fixture capability.

### H20 — Integrated machine assembly challenge
Create a compact automated drilling station with a 400 × 300 mm base, a vertical linear axis with 150 mm travel, a spindle envelope Ø80 mm, a motor envelope Ø100 × 160 mm, a vice fixture for a 100 × 80 × 20 mm workpiece, and a guarded chip-clearance envelope. Include linear rails, screw drive, bearing blocks, spindle bracket, workholding clamps, base stiffeners, limit-switch brackets, fasteners and an access panel. Define a consistent datum frame, component dependencies, mounting interfaces and a safe non-interfering home pose. Provide separate parts, assembled and exploded views, a STEP export and a validation report. Explicitly distinguish geometric validation from cutting-force, stiffness, safety and process validation.

